# Kernel space (amix-dlm C2-2/C2-3): catalogued in the #ext kpkg block, never
# as an ordinary entry; installs /stand/k30/{unix,kabi,unix.sym} and the
# cut's ufs and s5 modules at /etc/conf/mod.d (C2-3 v8 sec 1).  The module
# kernel (family generic, kabi k30.1.4), build id 68030-261106-72 (uname -m,
# kinfo); its own package, not a revision of kernel-k30-u (C2-3 sec 1.1).
APKG_DESC=The 68030 module kernel (kabi k30.1.4, loader 2): /stand/k30/unix (brel) + kabi + unix.sym, with no built-in ufs or s5: it carries the ufs and s5 modules built with it (/etc/conf/mod.d); drivers such as z3660eth come as module packages; kbootset composes them into a boot generation.  Upgrade together with kbootset -37 or later (its namelist is the one crash reads): a generation made by a kbootset older than -34 has no major records, so its module majors are refused (the box still boots), and since -35 `kbootset cards` reads hex controller numbers, so a disk on card 2 gets its own card's key.  New in kabi 1.3: /dev/paula support (the paula-k30 module package), and a /dev/audio open that fails at once (EIO) when Paula never interrupts instead of hanging the box.  New in -56: shrinking a big file (ftruncate, tar --delete, dd seek=) no longer panics the kernel; a file shrunk and then regrown sparsely no longer reads its old bytes back; a 68030 without an FPU no longer panics at the first signal.  New in -63: a STREAMS queue still scheduled is no longer freed under the scheduler, so a unix-socket server under client traffic (prngd with prngd-ctl) no longer panics the kernel.  New in -64: the kernel knows the random module's major (c 48), so the random module package (/dev/random, /dev/urandom) loads.  New in -65 (kabi 1.4): a filesystem module can be a demand module that loads at its first mount.  New in -66: loading a module on first use no longer waits for free memory; the copy of its file is freed before the load.  New in -67: adding a swap area too large for the kernel's map space (swap -a, or the tail of the swap slice at boot) fails with "Not enough space" instead of hanging; on a 64 MB machine about 50 MiB can be added beside the boot swap, on 16 MB about 248 MiB.  New in -68: a process's data and virtual memory are no longer limited to 16 MB by default (ulimit -d and -v unlimited; the default stack limit stays 16 MB), so it can use what memory and swap allow; module majors come from the boot generation (kbootset -34), so a new module major needs no new kernel.  New in -69: the kernel no longer walks the module interrupt chains while no module has attached a handler, which takes that cost off every clock tick and disk interrupt.  New in -70: a SCSI command sent through /dev/scsi as the first to the A3000's onboard SCSI no longer kills its driver (a3091: 0x1 0 0x0) and hangs, as kbootset inquiry from an installer did; the A1200's IDE port is the Gayle motherboard controller, as the A4000's, for a root on an A1200 IDE disk.  New in -71: sar, sadc, timex -s and crash work again: the kernel keeps the inode tables they read (no more "sadc cannot copy inode table"), and with kbootset -37 crash reads the namelist's timestamp and the loaded modules' symbols.  New in -72: the kernel's map space is 8 MiB instead of 4, which leaves room for kmem and swap areas on a large machine, and a large kmem request that had to wait is woken when memory is freed; one swap area may be at most 32 KiB per page of memory and must leave the map space 1 MiB free in one run (measured: 192 MiB works on 16 MB, 384 MiB on 32 and 64 MB), and a refused add takes about 4 s; touching memory a program may not access gives it a signal or EFAULT instead of hanging, tas and cas on a read-only page give SIGSEGV, and a core dump skips pages with no access; the A2065 no longer drops frames under heavy receive load (IP's read queue mark 0x10000).
DESC=The module kernel of the 030 line (the k30 kernel with ufs and s5 carved out): the line's kernel with kinfo and the amix-dlm module core (the module core of amix kernel/dlm 9cd652d: the four-card sd layer, the halt hook, the sd seam, bdevsw row 20 freed, the root pass: root-class modules loaded from the arena, dlm_marc_root, root_defer, iodone, ramdisk_image and vfs_register; the arena 16-aligned; the five DDI 1.2 DMA names with the core's 030 DMA provider; the 13 filesystem names and the 100 core-tier names the filesystem modules bind to; the safe boot modules; DDI minor 3, kabi k30.1.3: AllocMem, FreeMem, displaytype, paula_busy and audio_nopen for the paula module, the kernel listing only the names of its own minor; the major registry with c 48 random; kabi k30.1.4: a demand-class filesystem module loads at its first mount; a first-use load never waits for memory, and one short of memory prints the first-use line; a module's majors are the booted manifest's major records; an interrupt chain with no handler attached is not walked; the kmem readers' inode tables are the kernel's, and inode names an empty read-only table while s5 is not loaded) linked in, with the stock audio driver patched by amix-paula 1b2736d (MIT: while /dev/paula is open /dev/audio refuses its open and the other way round; a safe close after a killed player; the open's wait for Paula's first interrupt is bounded, so a silent Paula fails the open with EIO instead of spinning), with the 040/060 port's rth1 at 31edbc6 (AXL1's RAM-disk root selector and rootfstype, swap /dev/fakeswap on a RAM-disk root; a motherboard controller's root record matched by its kinfo id, rth1=2), cut from amix 9cd652d, with sdqueue at sdspl or above (the stock A3000 SCSI driver's first command from the raw passthrough runs with level 2 masked), with the STREAMS freeq guard (qdetach, strclose and stropen free a queue through freeq_guarded), the soft data and vmem limits unlimited, and the swapadd guard (swapadd allocates a swap area's anon array without sleeping and fails with ENOMEM when kernel virtual space has no run that large), shipped in the brel form the amix boot loader binds.  No static A4091/A4092/A4770, Gayle IDE or cdfs: they come as module packages (a4091-k30, gayle-k30, cdfs-k30); the kernel drives the A3000 SCSI and the A2090/A2091 itself (root= mobo:a3000-wd,zorro:0202:01,zorro:0202:03).  Build -71 has no built-in ufs or s5 filesystem: the package carries the ufs and s5 modules as core-tier modules built with this cut (/etc/conf/mod.d/ufs and s5, comment-only /etc/conf/modreg.d files), and the sidecar names no rootfs=; kbootset promotes the root filesystem's module to a root record.  /stand/k30/kabi is the kernel build's sidecar for the brel (kabi=k30.1.4, family=generic, loader-min=2, bss, arena, ident, ksym, abi=, sdcards=, rth1=2, sym=), copied verbatim; /stand/k30/unix.sym holds the brel's symbols for kbootset's namelist.  Upgrade it together with kbootset -37 or later (since -37 the namelist kbootset writes carries crash_sync's word and the loaded modules' globals, which crash reads, and the sidecar's kbootset-min refuses an older one; since -35 `kbootset cards` reads hex controller numbers, so a disk on card 2 (c10-c17) gets its own card's key; since -34 it writes the registry's major records into the manifest, which this kernel's module core reads a module's majors from, with c 48 random and c 55 zz9ksvc; a generation from an older kbootset has none, so its module majors are refused while the box still boots; since -22, amix-dlm 1308c0b: a motherboard root record by its kinfo id for an rth1=2 kernel, and the core-tier module's tier= and build= in the root record).  The package writes no boot store itself; after the install apkg runs kbootset build and commit (or prints the command when bootset=manual).
